 a tough cover \u2014 KC hold WR1s to just 50 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p>In the red zone KC throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Rashee Rice</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to KC \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p>A middling 48 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>KC \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>KC stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>50%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> (16 carries, 69 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though KC's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 64 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Travis Kelce</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 45 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>IND \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>IND are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (44% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 IND project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Jonathan Taylor</strong>'s rushing projection (15 carries, 73 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Alec Pierce</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, IND funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>5 targets for 46 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tyler Warren</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 44 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"IND","col":"#4F9BE8","players":[{"name":"Easton Stick","pos":"QB","starter":false,"minor":true,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":221.75,"book":null,"over_th":207.66,"under_th":231.5,"call":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":0.83,"book":null,"over_th":0.13,"under_th":0.32,"call":null,"edge":null}],"td":{"pct":21.1,"fair":"4/1","book":null,"value":null}},{"name":"Daniel Jones","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":74.35,"book":null,"over_th":61.14,"under_th":85.75,"cal